# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for CI's tests-tsan and tests-tsan-full presets
# (issue #1283).
#
# ubuntu-latest moved to ubuntu-26.04. Its PortAudio, 19.7.0+git20260206, links
# libpulse and brings up a PulseAudio host API. Pa_Initialize then starts
# pulse's threaded mainloop ('threaded-ml') even on a runner with no server.
# libpulsecommon is not built with -fsanitize=thread. TSan can't see the
# ordering pulse puts between a pa_mutex it pa_xmalloc()s on the caller's thread
# and the first pa_mutex_lock() on its own thread, so it reports a race inside
# the library. 24.04's PortAudio 19.6.0 had no PulseAudio host API, so this
# never happened there.
#
# Rules (same as tools/tsan/host-audio.supp, which repeats this entry for the
# local presets):
#   - Name a library function, never a YSE frame.
#   - No called_from_lib: for libpulsecommon. That would also hide its
#     malloc/free from TSan, and with them any YSE heap bug that lands in a
#     block pulse allocated.

# A pa_mutex created by pa_xmalloc on one thread and locked on pulse's
# mainloop thread. Both stacks are entirely inside libpulsecommon.
race:pa_mutex_lock
